{"name":"Gargantua","hex":"#eeee55","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[],"entries":[{"title":"Gargantua","blocks":[{"text":"La vie tres horrifique du grand Gargantua, père de Pantagruel jadis composée par M. Alcofribas abstracteur de quinte essence. Livre plein de Pantagruelisme according to François Juste's 1542 edition, or simply Gargantua, is the second novel by François Rabelais, published in 1534 or 1535."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/P%C3%A8lerins_mang%C3%A9s_en_salade_-_Gravure_de_Gustave_Dor%C3%A9_pour_le_chapitre_38_de_Gargantua.jpg/330px-P%C3%A8lerins_mang%C3%A9s_en_salade_-_Gravure_de_Gustave_Dor%C3%A9_pour_le_chapitre_38_de_Gargantua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gargantua","wikidata":"Q10288350"},{"title":"Gargantua (gorilla)","blocks":[{"text":"Gargantua was a captive western lowland gorilla famed for being exhibited by the Ringling Brothers circus. He has been credited with saving the business from bankruptcy. An acid scar on his face gave Gargantua a snarling, menacing expression, which the circus management exploited by generating publicity falsely exaggerating his purported hatred of humans. He was also claimed to be the largest gorilla in captivity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Gargantua2.jpg/330px-Gargantua2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gargantua_(gorilla)","wikidata":"Q5523168"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Gargantua02.jpg/500px-Gargantua02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Gargantua02.jpg/500px-Gargantua02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration of Gargantua by Gustave Doré (1873).","lin